  "raw_answer": "C1|The context only partially covers the main classes of antiarrhythmic drugs, describing class II (beta blockers) and class III (potassium‑channel blockers). |Passage 2|Passage 3|Passage 4  \nC2|Class II antiarrhythmic drugs (beta blockers) decrease SA and AV nodal activity by lowering cAMP and calcium currents, suppress abnormal pacemaker activity by reducing the phase‑4 slope, and increase PR interval. |Passage 2|Passage 4  \nC3|Class III antiarrhythmic drugs (e.g., amiodarone, ibutilide, dofetilide, sotalol) block the delayed‑rectifier potassium current (I_K), slowing phase 3 repolarization, which lengthens action‑potential duration, increases the effective refractory period, and prolongs the QT interval.",
